{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Visiting the iniquity of the fathers, etc.</b> The explanation is, since He said that HaShem is zealous (קַנַּאי), may He be blessed, and it is known that zealousness would normally be activated immediately upon the thing that provokes the zealousness — yet we see the negation of this, heaven forbid: how many and how many have rebelled and transgressed and done [evil] and succeeded, and the hand of the zealous one did not touch them. Therefore He said <b>פּוֹקֵד</b> ('visiting') — meaning, if you see the contrary of zealousness, know that 'I am פּוֹקֵד' — meaning, this is My attribute, that I do not exact payment from evildoers immediately, but I extend My patience until the second and third and fourth generation — that is to say, at times — and I do not extend My patience beyond this time. And the reason is, for if HaShem were to be zealous against a person immediately, there would be no endurance for the created beings; therefore HaShem acts wisely with evildoers and shuts His eye from destroying them in this world, so that their children may arise — perhaps they will improve their ways, and thus the world endures through them. This also benefits the fathers, to merit them in the World to Come, as they said (Sanhedrin 104a): 'A son merits a father.' And if the second generation acted wickedly like the deeds of the fathers, then He visits upon them the iniquity of the fathers — they perish together — and the zealousness against the evil of the father is still realized through the punishment of the sons. And at times HaShem will further extend His patience even to the second generation and suspend [punishment] until the third generation, for the same reason stated above, and when they act wickedly He visits upon them, etc. And at times He will further extend His patience even to the third generation as well, but for the fourth generation there is no longer any hope for them, for they have taken root in the klippah and will not be severed from it, and the zealousness of HaShem will burn against them — their iniquity and the iniquity of the fathers of the fathers of their fathers, from the first generation, shall be destroyed. And the reason is that all of them are one chain of the aspect of evil. And I will give you insight, that in the reckoning of the Master, may He be blessed, even when He extends His patience until the fourth generation, it is called immediate, for three and four generations are three hundred years, and a day of the Holy One, blessed be He, is a thousand years — and just as you would say 'immediately' in human reckoning [is] six or seven hours, so too in the reckoning of the Master three hundred years [is regarded the same way]. And through this you will find a resolution for why HaShem saw fit to say <b>upon</b> and <b>upon</b> rather than saying in general terms 'פּוֹקֵד עֲוֹן עַד רִבֵּיעִים' ('visiting iniquity until the fourth'), as He said regarding those who do kindness 'to thousands' — for this differs from that. Understand this, and see what follows:" "<b>And know</b> that contemplation of this matter — that HaShem does not act with zealousness against evildoers — is an awesome thing, all the more so [when] a wicked person extends himself in his wickedness and succeeds at the height of success. And one who is wise concerning the boundary that the Creator set, namely four generations, will justify the judgment of justice. And this is what Asaph said (Psalms 73:17): 'אָבִינָה לְאַחֲרִיתָם' ('I discerned their end') — meaning, the end of the fixed limit of generations that HaShem set. And know that the power of the aspect of evil exists for up to four generations, and HaShem does not exact payment from a fifth generation for the iniquity of the original fathers — which would be the fifth from them — unlike the aspect of good, which grows to a thousand and to thousands."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{